  "query_preview": "../Wednesday through Sunday/...The long term forecast \nremains largely uneventful. Clearing skies will continue take \nover the rest of the panhandle from north to south Wednesday. The \nlow pressure system bringing rainfall to the southern panhandle \nTuesday meanders southward through Wednesday, continuing a chance \nof scattered showers for parts of the southern and central \npanhandle through the afternoon. Shower potential will diminish \ninto Thursday as an area of ridging shifts eastward through the \ngulf, persisting through Saturday and bringing northwesterly flow,\ndrier conditions, and warmer temperatures through the second half\nof the week. High temperatures will reach the high 60s to low 70s\nin the northern panhandle and the low to mid 70s in the southern \npanhandle Thursday and Friday with ample clearing.\n\nNortherly winds will increase up to 25 mph through Lynn Canal and\ninto Northern Chatham Strait overnight from Tuesday into \nWednesday, decrease through the day, and increase again through \nThursday morning. Winds will decrease again overnight into Friday \nmorning before the flow switches southerly through the afternoon \nas the pressure gradient turns. Models are in high disagreement \ngoing into the weekend, with the EC holding onto the ridge in the \ngulf through the weekend and the GFS favoring an upper level \nshortwave making it over the ridge and into the northern panhandle\nSaturday. Depending on how things change over the next few days, \nwe could have a very dry or very wet weekend.",
